&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 0&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50000115</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 23:23:04 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Show HN: StayLeet – keeping skills sharp in AI coding era</title><link>https://stayleet.com/</link><description>&lt;p&gt;As AI writes more and more of the code, it's quite important to maintain sufficient skill and knowledge to even review or judge AI's output.&lt;p&gt;Here is a little project I whipped up - I personally found deliberate practice quite useful.  It focusses on non-coding skills (as I think LOC production is basically solved by AI, IMO).  Rather I start with two tracks: general technical fluency (CS / systems / practical fundamentals), and system design.&lt;p&gt;And yes it was built primarily with help of AI tooling.&lt;p&gt;Would love to get some feedback - in particular, do others feel the same way about skill atrophy risk on-the-job nowadays, and whether this tool is helpful potentially or not.  What else do folks do instead, if anything.&lt;/p&gt;
